# MELPA Compliance Standards

This rule enforces MELPA submission and maintenance standards based on [MELPA CONTRIBUTING.org](https://github.com/melpa/melpa/blob/master/CONTRIBUTING.org).

## Code Quality Requirements

### Lexical Binding
- **Always enable lexical binding** in every `.el` file:
  ```elisp
  ;;; package.el --- Description -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
  ```

### Function References
- Use `#'function-name` (function form) instead of `'function-name` (quote form) for function references:
  ```elisp
  ;; ❌ BAD
  (mapcar 'my-function list)
  
  ;; ✅ GOOD
  (mapcar #'my-function list)
  ```

### Function Length
- **Avoid long functions**: Break down functions longer than ~50 lines into smaller, well-named helper functions
- Each function should have a single, clear responsibility
- Use descriptive function names with package prefix (e.g., `cursor-agent--helper-function`)

### Face Definitions
- **Never** define a face that both `:inherit` another face AND override attributes (bold, underline, inverse-video)
- Best practice: Only `:inherit` faces and let users customize attributes

## Package Metadata

### Required Header Fields
Every package must have proper header metadata:
```elisp
;;; package-name.el --- Description -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-

;; Author: Name
;; Maintainer: Name or GitHub username
;; Version: X.Y.Z
;; Package-Requires: ((emacs "27.1") (dependency "version"))
;; Keywords: keyword1, keyword2
;; URL: https://github.com/user/package-name
```

### Package Requirements
- Package name must match filename (without `.el`)
- `Package-Requires` must list all dependencies
- Version format: `X.Y.Z` (semantic versioning)

## Documentation Standards

### Docstrings
- Use `checkdoc` to verify documentation strings
- Error messages should **NOT** end with periods
- Error messages should start with capital letters
- Use two spaces after periods in docstrings (Emacs convention)
- Quote Lisp symbols in docstrings: `` `symbol-name' ``

```elisp
;; ❌ BAD
(defun my-function ()
  "Does something. Returns a value.")

;; ✅ GOOD
(defun my-function ()
  "Does something.  Returns a value.")
```

### Error Messages
```elisp
;; ❌ BAD
(user-error "Something went wrong.")

;; ✅ GOOD
(user-error "Something went wrong")
```

## Code Style

### Emacs Lisp Conventions
- Follow [Emacs Lisp Style Guide](https://github.com/bbatsov/emacs-lisp-style-guide)
- Follow [Emacs Lisp Conventions](https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/elisp/Tips.html)
- Use `package-lint` to check package metadata
- Use `flycheck` or `flymake` for syntax checking

### Autoload Cookies
- Use `;;;###autoload` for interactive commands and user-facing functions
- Place autoload cookie directly before the `defun` or `defcustom`

## License Requirements

### GPL Compatible License
- Must use GPL-compatible license (preferably GPL v3)
- License boilerplate must appear **above** `;;; Commentary:` section
- Repository must contain `LICENSE` or `COPYING` file
- License file must be detectable by common tooling (e.g., licensee)

### License Header Format
```elisp
;; This program is free software: you can redistribute it and/or modify
;; it under the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by
;; the Free Software Foundation, either version 3 of the License, or
;; (at your option) any later version.
```

## File Organization

### Single File Packages
- MELPA only looks at the main `.el` file (or `-pkg.el` if provided, though discouraged)
- If you have optional parts with different dependencies, publish them as **separate packages**

### README Files
- Avoid README files unless they have interactive commands to display them
- If README exists, provide an interactive command: `M-x package-name-readme`
- Exception: `.info` files are automatically added to Emacs info index

## Quality Checks

### Before Submitting
Always run these tools:
1. **package-lint**: `M-x package-lint-batch-and-exit`
2. **checkdoc**: `M-x checkdoc-file`
3. **flycheck/flymake**: For syntax errors

### Common Issues to Fix
- [ ] Lexical binding enabled
- [ ] No periods in error messages
- [ ] Function references use `#'` not `'`
- [ ] Long functions broken down
- [ ] Package metadata complete and correct
- [ ] License header above Commentary section
- [ ] All dependencies in Package-Requires
- [ ] Docstrings follow Emacs conventions

## Testing

### Recipe Testing
Before submitting to MELPA:
```bash
# Build the recipe
make recipes/package-name

# Test in sandbox
make sandbox INSTALL=package-name
```

### Installation Testing
- Test package installation: `M-x package-install-file`
- Verify all interactive commands work
- Test in both GUI and terminal Emacs (if applicable)

## Repository Requirements

### SCM Requirements
- Must be in Git or Mercurial repository
- Must be the official repository (not a fork, except extreme circumstances)
- Each package in its own dedicated repository
- Repository must be public and accessible

### Version Tags
- Use semantic versioning tags for stable releases
- Tag format compatible with `version-to-list` function
- Example: `v1.0.0` or `1.0.0`

## Maintenance

### Recipe Maintenance
- Update recipe if repository moves
- Use GitHub repository transfer mechanism when possible
- Update README at old location with link to new location

### Active Maintenance
- Package should have reasonably active maintainer
- MELPA is not a code dump - maintainers should respond to issues
- Life happens, but reasonable maintenance efforts expected

## Examples

### Good Function Structure
```elisp
(defun cursor-agent--helper-function (arg)
  "Helper function that does one specific thing.
ARG is the argument to process.
Returns processed result."
  ;; Clear, focused implementation
  )

(defun cursor-agent-main-function ()
  "Main function that orchestrates the work."
  (let ((result (cursor-agent--helper-function arg)))
    ;; Use helper results
    ))
```

### Good Error Handling
```elisp
(defun my-function ()
  "Does something important."
  (unless (condition)
    (user-error "Condition not met.  Please check setup"))
  ;; Continue...
  )
```
